{
  "summary": "This article introduces Tim Costello, a well-known Australian Baptist minister and social advocate, now 69 years old. As a student in Year 9 (around age 14), he asked his teacher whether poverty is natural and unavoidable — like the tides or sunrise. His teacher’s answer — that poverty is not natural, but created by human decisions and policies — shaped his life’s work. Since then, he’s spent over 30 years fighting poverty in Australia and around the world, including in war zones and disaster areas. He’s also served as mayor of St Kilda (a Melbourne suburb), written books, and been named a National Living Treasure. Though often mistaken for his more politically prominent brother, Peter Costello (a former federal treasurer and Liberal Party figure), Tim is best known for his humanitarian leadership — especially his 13-year role as CEO of World Vision Australia. The article paints a picture of him reflecting quietly on Frankston beach, underscoring how deeply personal and purpose-driven his lifelong mission against poverty is."
}
```

